libc++: steady_clock over the ABSD kit's tick counter

Failure: libc++ built with threads (the external thread API, which the
LLVM profile needs for std::mutex) requires the monotonic clock
(libcxx/CMakeLists.txt: LIBCXX_ENABLE_MONOTONIC_CLOCK may be OFF only with
threads OFF), and chrono.cpp then stops with
`#error "Monotonic clock not implemented on this platform"`: Picolibc
defines _POSIX_TIMERS only for RTEMS and the kit has no clock_gettime.
Change: under _LIBCPP_ABSD_KIT (set by scripts/build-cxx-runtime.sh,
profile llvm, only), steady_clock::now() returns the kit's times() tick
count as milliseconds: the kernel's boot tick counter, monotonic
(C-INTERFACE.md section 7: one tick is about 0.99985 ms, counted from
boot; the kit's clock() makes the same ms reading).
Behaviour preserved: every other platform's branch is unchanged;
system_clock keeps its existing gettimeofday branch on the kit.
Upstreamability: low as written (a lane-local macro); an upstream ABSD
port would key this on the target OS macro and a kit clock_gettime.

--- a/libcxx/src/chrono.cpp
+++ b/libcxx/src/chrono.cpp
@@ -27,6 +27,10 @@
 #  include <unistd.h> // _POSIX_TIMERS
 #endif
 
+#if defined(_LIBCPP_ABSD_KIT)
+#  include <sys/times.h> // times()
+#endif
+
 #if __has_include(<sys/time.h>)
 #  include <sys/time.h> // for gettimeofday and timeval
 #endif
@@ -212,6 +216,15 @@
   return steady_clock::time_point(nanoseconds(_zx_clock_get_monotonic()));
 }
 
+#  elif defined(_LIBCPP_ABSD_KIT)
+
+// The ABSD C kit has no clock_gettime; its monotonic clock is the kernel's
+// boot tick count, which times() returns (C-INTERFACE.md section 7).
+static steady_clock::time_point __libcpp_steady_clock_now() {
+  struct tms __unused_times;
+  return steady_clock::time_point(milliseconds(static_cast<long long>(times(&__unused_times))));
+}
+
 #  elif defined(_LIBCPP_HAS_CLOCK_GETTIME)
 
 static steady_clock::time_point __libcpp_steady_clock_now() {
